```java
// for — when iteration count is known
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

// while — when condition drives the loop
int x = 10;
while (x > 0) {
    System.out.println(x--);
}

// do-while — body runs AT LEAST ONCE
int choice;
do {
    choice = getUserChoice();
} while (choice != 0);
```

The critical difference: `while` and `for` check the condition **before** entering the loop. `do-while` runs the body first, then checks the condition.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;  // skip 3, next iteration
    if (i == 7) break;     // stop loop entirely at 7
    System.out.println(i); // prints 0,1,2,4,5,6
}

// return exits the whole method
int findFirst(int[] arr, int target) {
    for (int x : arr) {
        if (x == target) return x;  // exits method immediately
    }
    return -1;
}
```

**`break`** in a switch statement exits that case block. In a loop, it exits the entire loop.

**`continue`** only applies to loops — it skips the rest of the current iteration's body.

**`return`** exits the current method, regardless of how deeply nested in loops.

```java
int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};

// Traditional for — when you need index
for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(numbers[i]);
}

// Enhanced for-each — cleaner for reading
for (int num : numbers) {
    System.out.println(num);
}

// Works with any Iterable (List, Set, etc.)
List<String> names = List.of("Alice", "Bob");
for (String name : names) {
    System.out.println(name);
}
```

**When NOT to use for-each**:
- When you need the current index
- When you need to modify array elements (assignment to loop variable doesn't modify source)
- When iterating two collections simultaneously

```java
// if-else — flexible, handles ranges and complex conditions
if (score >= 90) grade = "A";
else if (score >= 80) grade = "B";
else grade = "C";

// Traditional switch — discrete values, needs break
switch (day) {
    case "MON": System.out.println("Monday"); break;
    case "TUE": System.out.println("Tuesday"); break;
    default: System.out.println("Other");
}

// Java 14+ switch expression — no fall-through risk
String result = switch (day) {
    case "MON" -> "Monday";
    case "TUE" -> "Tuesday";
    default -> "Other";
};
```

**Switch fall-through**: In traditional switch, if you forget `break`, execution falls through to the next case — this is a common bug but sometimes used intentionally.

```java
// Prints a multiplication table — outer × inner = n² iterations
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
        System.out.print(i * j + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
// Output: 1 2 3 / 2 4 6 / 3 6 9

// Breaking out of nested loop with label
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
        if (i == 2 && j == 2) break outer; // exits BOTH loops
    }
}
```

A plain `break` only exits the **innermost** loop. To exit outer loops, use a **labeled break**.
